course: everyday
lesson: 494
title: Shouty parents and bad days
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In Leicester, Jordan knocks his juice over and Dad yells, «For God's sake, can't you be careful?!» Jordan jumps. An hour later Dad sits on his bed: «Sorry, mate. Long day. Shouldn't have yelled.» Grown-ups get tired and stressed just like kids, and sometimes they snap. Shouting isn't OK, but a sorry shows they know it. In Sheffield, Lyra asks, «Can we go to the park?» and Mum, mardy (moody) after a long shift, snaps, «Not now!» Lyra thinks she's done something wrong, but the snap usually isn't about you. Later, «Bad day, Mum?» can help.
explain: Some shouting isn't angry at all. In Derry, Seamus's da roars at the telly, «Ye useless eejit!» («eejit» is Irish for idiot) and Seamus's friend jumps. «Relax, he's at the ref, not us.» In Liverpool, Mum yells, «Clean your room, it's a tip!», then later, «C'mere, ye little muppet» («muppet» here means a silly one, said with love) and squeezes Molly-Rae tight. In Adelaide, Kayden's mum shouts from the side of the footy pitch, «Kayden, you drongo, run!» («drongo» is Aussie for a fool, here in fun), and his mates laugh; if it bugs him, he can tell her privately. Loud and warm can go together.
explain: In Philadelphia, Cody's friend hears his mom yell, «Get your butt in here NOW!» «Yo, your mom's scary.» «Nah, she's just loud.» Some families are just loud, and loud isn't always angry or dangerous. In Birmingham, after a row about homework, Dhruv's mum shouts and his dad says nothing all evening, and Dhruv doesn't know which is worse. Some people shout when they're angry and some go quiet; both feel bad, and it helps when grown-ups talk it through. Dhruv can ask, «Are we okay?»
explain: Sometimes money or worry is behind the shouting. In Belfast, Megan loses her bus pass and Mum shouts, then cries in the kitchen. Megan feels guilty. She can say sorry for the bus pass; the rest is grown-up money stress, and it isn't her fault. In Toronto, Zelda tiptoes when Dad gets home from work and checks his face first, to see what mood he's in. That's a lot for a child to carry. She can talk to her mum or a teacher, because no child should have to tiptoe every day.
explain: Sometimes it's frightening. In Glasgow, the car breaks down and Aimee's dad loses it and kicks the tyre, and Aimee goes very still in the back seat. Kicking a tyre is anger at a thing, not at a person, but it still feels scary to see. Knowing the difference matters: shouting at a match or about a messy room is one thing; anger that turns on people, or feeling scared to go home, is another. Then a child should tell a trusted adult, like a teacher, or call Childline on 0800 1111 (free, in the UK), any time, day or night.
fact: Grown-ups get tired and stressed and sometimes snap; it usually isn't about the child.
fact: Shouting isn't OK, but a parent saying sorry afterwards shows they know it.
fact: Some families are just loud, and loud isn't always angry or dangerous.
fact: Shouting at the telly or at a football match is common and is not aimed at the people at home.
fact: Some parents are shouty and very warm at the same time.
fact: Some people shout when they're angry and some go quiet; it helps when grown-ups talk it through, and a child can ask «Are we okay?».
fact: Money worries can make parents snap; the stress is grown-up business and not the child's fault.
fact: Watching a parent's mood all the time and tiptoeing is a lot to carry; a child can talk to the other parent or a teacher.
fact: Seeing a parent lose their temper, even at a thing like a tyre, can be frightening.
fact: If a parent's anger turns on people, or a child is scared to go home, the child should tell a trusted adult like a teacher.
fact: Childline is a free UK helpline for children, on 0800 1111, open any time.
fact: If a parent's joke-shouting in public bugs you, you can tell them privately.
fact: Words in this lesson: «mardy» (Midlands, North) means moody, «eejit» (Ireland) means idiot, «muppet» (UK) means a silly person, often said fondly, «drongo» (Australia) means a fool, «it's a tip» means it's a mess, «lose it» means lose your temper.
exercise: Jordan spilled juice and Dad yelled. What did Dad do later? | Said sorry | Yelled again | Bought more juice | Went to bed
exercise: Why do grown-ups sometimes snap? | They're tired or stressed | They enjoy making kids cry | The child is always to blame | Shouting is good for the lungs
exercise: Mum snapped «Not now!» at Lyra. What was it really about? | Mum's long, tiring shift | Lyra being a really bad girl | The park being closed | Mum hating parks
exercise: «Mardy after a long shift.» What does «mardy» mean? | Moody | Hungry | Sleepy | Happy
exercise: What could Lyra try later? | «Bad day, Mum?» | Asking ten more times | Going to the park alone | Shouting back louder
exercise: Seamus's da roared «Ye useless eejit!» Who at? | The referee on the telly | Seamus's friend on the sofa | Seamus, for being too loud | The dog in the kitchen
exercise: «C'mere, ye little muppet,» then a squeeze. How does Molly-Rae's mum feel about her? | She loves her | She's still cross | She thinks she's a puppet | She wants her to go
exercise: «Kayden, you drongo, run!» What is a drongo? | A fool, said here in fun | A very fast runner | A kind of football boot | A coach who shouts a lot
exercise: If his mum's shouting at the footy bugs Kayden, what can he do? | Tell her privately | Shout back at the match | Stop playing football | Pretend he's not her kid
exercise: «Nah, she's just loud.» What does Cody mean about his mom? | Loud isn't the same as angry | She is very angry all day | She's scary to everyone | She can't hear very well at all
exercise: Dhruv's mum shouts and his dad goes silent. What can Dhruv ask? | «Are we okay?» | «Who's winning?» | «Can I have pizza?» | «Who's the worst?»
exercise: Why did Megan's mum cry after shouting about the bus pass? | Money stress, not Megan | She hates buses | Megan is a bad kid | She'd lost her own pass too
exercise: What can Megan do about the bus pass row? | Say sorry for the pass, and know the rest isn't her fault | Pay Mum back for every bus ride with her pocket money | Stop going on buses so Mum never has to buy another | Blame her little brother so Mum isn't cross with her
exercise: Zelda checks Dad's face every evening before she talks. What's going on? | She's become a worried mood watcher | She's practising for a drawing test | Dad asked her to check his face daily | She thinks he has a spot on his nose
exercise: Who could Zelda talk to? | Her mum or a teacher | Nobody, it's a secret | Dad's boss at work | A stranger online
exercise: Aimee's dad kicked the tyre. Was his anger aimed at a person? | No, at a thing, but it was still scary to see | Yes, at Aimee, for sitting in the back seat | Yes, at the man in the car behind them all | No, so Aimee shouldn't have felt anything
exercise: What is Childline's number in the UK? | 0800 1111 | 999 | 0800 0000 | 111
test: «if ur parent shouts it means they hate u». True? | No, shouting usually comes from stress | Yes, shouting always means hate | Yes, unless they buy you sweets | No, it means they're deaf
test: Seamus's friend: «your da's lost it, he's screaming». Seamus: «he's at the ref». What's the difference that matters? | Shouting at a game isn't shouting at people | Shouting at a game is always more dangerous | There's no difference, all shouting's the same | Shouting at the telly means he's cross at Seamus
test: A friend says, «my dad gets so angry at us I'm scared to go home». What should the friend do? | Tell a trusted adult or call Childline | Keep it secret so Dad doesn't find out | Stay at the park until it's dark outside | Wait for it to get better by itself
test: In a cartoon, the mum shouts «DINNER!» so loud the house shakes and everyone laughs. What's real about it? | Some families are just loud, and that's fine | Every loud mum is secretly very dangerous | Shouting makes houses fall down for real | Loud families are always very unhappy ones
test: Megan: «Mum cried cos of me, it's all my fault». What's true? | She lost the pass, but the money stress isn't her fault | It's all her fault, she should feel bad for days | It's nobody's fault that she lost her bus pass | Mum cried because she wanted a new bus pass
test: What would probably happen if Zelda kept tiptoeing every night for years and told nobody? | She'd carry a lot of worry on her own | Dad would be in a good mood for ever | She'd become a famous ballet dancer | The worry would vanish by itself one day
test: Molly-Rae's mate: «your mum called you a muppet, that's well harsh». What's true? | Her mum said it fondly with a hug | Her mum thinks she's a TV puppet | Her mum was really, really angry | It's the worst thing a mum can say
test: Cody: «she yells «get your butt in here» like ten times a day but she's cool». Which shows something different from just loud? | A child who's scared to go home | A mum who yells across the house | A mum who shouts at dinner time | A family that's always noisy
test: Jordan's dad yelled at 6, said sorry at 7. Jordan's mate: «a sorry doesn't count if it's an hour late». Right? | No, a sorry later still counts | Yes, sorries only count straight away | Yes, it only counts with a present | No, sorries never count at all
test: Kayden: «Mum, don't call me a drongo in front of the lads». Where is the best place to say it? | At home, just the two of them | On the pitch, through a megaphone | In the group chat with all his mates | At school assembly in front of all
test: Aimee: «Dad only kicked the tyre, not me, so I shouldn't feel scared». What's true? | It's normal to feel scared seeing anger, even at a thing | She's silly to feel scared, nobody got hurt at all | She should kick the tyre too so she feels braver | She must never get in the car with her dad again
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the cause of a parent's occasional shouting? | It commonly arises from fatigue or stress | It invariably arises from hatred of the child | It is always the fault of the child concerned | It arises from a defect of the parent's hearing
exam: Identify the principal distinction drawn in the lesson between forms of shouting. | Shouting at a match or about mess differs from anger turned on people | Shouting at a match is invariably more dangerous than other shouting | All shouting is identical in its cause, its object and its effect | Shouting is acceptable only when directed at one's own children at home
exam: Given that a child is afraid to return home on account of a parent's anger, ascertain the most appropriate course. | To inform a trusted adult or telephone Childline | To conceal the matter from every adult | To remain outdoors until after nightfall | To await improvement without speaking
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning parental distress arising from financial difficulty. | It is a grown-up matter and not the child's fault | It is wholly the fault of the child who lost an item | It is remedied by the child paying for all fares | It is caused by the child's choice of transport
